WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.800
- Well, it's Election Day, again.

00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:05.410
- Well, it's Groundhog Day, again.

00:00:05.410 --> 00:00:07.010
- And it seems like, every four years,

00:00:07.010 --> 00:00:08.350
we have the same conversations

00:00:08.350 --> 00:00:09.810
about the Electoral College

00:00:09.810 --> 00:00:11.140
because there are a lot of misconceptions

00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:14.030
about what the Electoral
College is and why it exists,

00:00:14.030 --> 00:00:16.290
what the problems with
the Electoral College are,

00:00:16.290 --> 00:00:18.650
and what we can possibly do about them.

00:00:18.650 --> 00:00:21.570
Contrary to popular belief,
citizens don't actually vote

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:23.730
for the president of the
United States directly.

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:26.068
And the Electoral College is the electors

00:00:26.068 --> 00:00:28.550
that form every four years

00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:31.680
to actually vote for who is
going to be the president

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:33.590
and vice-president of the United States.

00:00:33.590 --> 00:00:36.510
The Electoral College
consists of 538 electors

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:38.460
that are divvied up amongst the states

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:40.070
And in order to win the presidency,

00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:41.950
you have to receive an absolute majority

00:00:41.950 --> 00:00:44.680
of those electoral votes
which, at the present time,

00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:48.480
requires 270 votes in
the Electoral College.

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:49.890
You'll often hear people talk about

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:51.320
how you're not actually voting

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:53.010
for the president of the United States.

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:55.600
You're voting for people
to vote for the president

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:56.433
of the United States.

00:00:56.433 --> 00:00:58.040
And is that actually true?

00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:00.146
Well, we're going to get
into all of that today.

00:01:00.146 --> 00:01:04.140
(assertive horns music)

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:05.760
The basis for the Electoral College

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:08.700
is found in Article II,
Section I of the Constitution.

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:09.857
It gives each state, quote,

00:01:09.857 --> 00:01:12.677
"In such manner as the
legislature thereof may direct

00:01:12.677 --> 00:01:15.570
"electors equal to its
representation in Congress."

00:01:15.570 --> 00:01:17.550
And to eliminate the Electoral College,

00:01:17.550 --> 00:01:19.660
we would need to amend the Constitution.

00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:21.410
However, the Constitution and the courts

00:01:21.410 --> 00:01:23.580
give the states significant
leeway to make changes

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:26.210
to how their Electoral College
representatives are chosen

00:01:26.210 --> 00:01:28.090
and who they are required to vote for.

00:01:28.090 --> 00:01:30.830
So let's examine how all of this works.

00:01:30.830 --> 00:01:33.230
The framers' main concern
was that people wouldn't know

00:01:33.230 --> 00:01:34.400
who the national candidates were.

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:36.100
They'd be familiar with
their local candidates

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:38.260
but wouldn't care about
the national candidates.

00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:40.730
So there needed to be some
sort of intermediary body

00:01:40.730 --> 00:01:43.620
to make the decisions about
the national candidates.

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:46.280
Each state has its own
process for picking electors.

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:47.910
Democrats and Republicans,
at the state level,

00:01:47.910 --> 00:01:49.350
pick their own slate of electors

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:52.610
and this can happen in early
spring or as late as October.

00:01:52.610 --> 00:01:54.390
When each state certifies
its election results,

00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:55.490
that's when the winning party's

00:01:55.490 --> 00:01:57.450
slate of electors is officially chosen.

00:01:57.450 --> 00:01:58.540
And although I guess you could say

00:01:58.540 --> 00:01:59.920
it's an honor to be an elector,

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:01.160
traditionally, all the electors do

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:02.930
is rubber stamp the election results.

00:02:02.930 --> 00:02:04.660
Electors can be just about anyone.

00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:07.550
Bill Clinton was an elector
for New York in 2016.

00:02:07.550 --> 00:02:10.200
The Missouri Republican Party
nominated a guy in the past

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:12.750
who has been convicted of
violence at an abortion clinic.

00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:15.140
Michigan's Republicans
selected William Rauwerdink

00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:17.320
who engineered a huge
accounting fraud scandal

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:20.450
that required him to pay nearly
$300 million in restitution.

00:02:20.450 --> 00:02:23.030
In California, Nancy Pelosi's
daughter was an elector

00:02:23.030 --> 00:02:25.530
and so was Dianne
Feinstein's granddaughter.

00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:27.640
Like I said, it could
be just about anyone.

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:30.140
But the presidential election
is held every four years

00:02:30.140 --> 00:02:33.020
on the Tuesday after the
first Monday in November.

00:02:33.020 --> 00:02:35.450
In each state, electors meet
after the presidential election

00:02:35.450 --> 00:02:38.430
on the first Monday after the
second Wednesday in December

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:40.080
to cast their votes for the president

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:42.040
and vice-president in separate ballots.

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:44.170
And traditionally it doesn't
really matter who is picked

00:02:44.170 --> 00:02:46.270
since the electors are
supposed to pledge their vote

00:02:46.270 --> 00:02:48.120
to whomever the vote shows.

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:50.310
So if New York voters
decided they preferred

00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:52.680
hometown hero Donald
Trump to Hillary Clinton,

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:53.890
Bill Clinton, in 2016,

00:02:53.890 --> 00:02:56.610
would have had to cast his
electoral vote for Trump,

00:02:56.610 --> 00:02:58.260
with obvious exceptions.

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:00.410
Which takes us to faithless electors.

00:03:00.410 --> 00:03:02.290
Most states use the popular vote results

00:03:02.290 --> 00:03:05.470
to decide which party's slate
of electors will be in play.

00:03:05.470 --> 00:03:07.300
This means that if the
state requires electors

00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:09.030
to follow the popular vote in the state,

00:03:09.030 --> 00:03:11.010
and not all states do, then the electors

00:03:11.010 --> 00:03:13.010
must pledge their vote
to whichever candidate

00:03:13.010 --> 00:03:14.910
received the most votes in that state.

00:03:14.910 --> 00:03:17.630
And occasionally an elector
will assert their right

00:03:17.630 --> 00:03:19.110
to vote for any candidate they want,

00:03:19.110 --> 00:03:21.060
ignoring their party's candidate.

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:23.470
These people are known
as faithless electors

00:03:23.470 --> 00:03:26.210
and as of now, 33 states
and the District of Columbia

00:03:26.210 --> 00:03:29.010
have laws punishing electors
who decide to do this.

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:31.260
But only 15 of these states actually have

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:33.670
an enforcement mechanism
to force those electors

00:03:33.670 --> 00:03:36.400
to change their vote to
whoever won the popular vote.

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:40.710
And before 2016, there were
only 157 faithless electors

00:03:40.710 --> 00:03:42.080
in American history.

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:44.150
But this is becoming a
slightly larger problem

00:03:44.150 --> 00:03:45.020
as time goes on.

00:03:45.020 --> 00:03:46.836
In 2016, a few electors in Colorado

00:03:46.836 --> 00:03:49.620
cast votes instead for Colin Powell

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:50.800
who was not on the ballot,

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:53.530
even though Colorado voters
selected Hillary Clinton.

00:03:53.530 --> 00:03:55.330
In Washington State, a few electors voted

00:03:55.330 --> 00:03:57.390
for Colin Powell and John Kasich.

00:03:57.390 --> 00:03:59.960
Washington State's voters
selected Hillary Clinton.

00:03:59.960 --> 00:04:01.840
So what were these
faithless electors doing

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:03.660
and what were they hoping to accomplish?

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:05.320
Well, the electors who spoke on the record

00:04:05.320 --> 00:04:07.020
said that they wanted
to encourage Republicans

00:04:07.020 --> 00:04:09.560
to pick someone other than
Donald Trump as president.

00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:10.880
This is, of course, problematic

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:12.850
given that the election
had already happened

00:04:12.850 --> 00:04:14.900
and their state had
picked Hillary Clinton,

00:04:14.900 --> 00:04:17.750
but the Electoral College had
also already been settled,

00:04:17.750 --> 00:04:19.920
selecting Donald Trump as president.

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:21.970
So this seems as good of a time as any

00:04:21.970 --> 00:04:25.530
to pause and talk about some
of the anti-democratic issues

00:04:25.530 --> 00:04:28.150
inherent in the Electoral College.

00:04:28.150 --> 00:04:29.780
Obviously, we've seen several times now

00:04:29.780 --> 00:04:31.340
where one candidate it has received

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:34.220
more votes than the other
candidate on a national level,

00:04:34.220 --> 00:04:37.070
yet that candidate loses
the presidential election

00:04:37.070 --> 00:04:38.860
because of the way that the electors

00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:41.020
in the Electoral College are divvied up.

00:04:41.020 --> 00:04:43.540
Now, I can already hear many people saying

00:04:43.540 --> 00:04:45.297
and typing into the comments right now,

00:04:45.297 --> 00:04:49.210
"Yes, but America is not a
democracy, it is a republic."

00:04:49.210 --> 00:04:50.950
And that's true as far as it goes

00:04:50.950 --> 00:04:52.890
but it doesn't really
answer any of the questions.

00:04:52.890 --> 00:04:54.180
It's sort of like saying that

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:56.600
the First Amendment doesn't
protect all types of speech

00:04:56.600 --> 00:04:57.990
and that is also true,

00:04:57.990 --> 00:05:00.239
but it sort of raises the
question without answering

00:05:00.239 --> 00:05:03.630
what speech is protected and
what speech isn't protected.

00:05:03.630 --> 00:05:06.880
And here, where you're talking
about a democratic republic

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:10.770
or a Republican democracy,
it's a bit of a spectrum.

00:05:10.770 --> 00:05:14.330
And the American system
incorporates aspects of a republic

00:05:14.330 --> 00:05:17.260
as it does incorporate aspects
of a democracy as well.

00:05:17.260 --> 00:05:18.400
And it doesn't really answer the question

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:20.280
as to which one is better.

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:21.850
And in fairness to those people who argue

00:05:21.850 --> 00:05:23.950
that America is republican not a democracy

00:05:23.950 --> 00:05:26.260
to say something is pro-democratic

00:05:26.260 --> 00:05:28.089
isn't necessarily a good thing.

00:05:28.089 --> 00:05:31.940
Reasons why you want to
incorporate republican principles

00:05:31.940 --> 00:05:33.280
and generally what people mean

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:35.390
when they say something is republican,

00:05:35.390 --> 00:05:37.250
that's small-R republican,

00:05:37.250 --> 00:05:39.040
What they're saying is that
there is an intermediary

00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:39.907
between the people who vote

00:05:39.907 --> 00:05:41.620
and the people that make the laws.

00:05:41.620 --> 00:05:44.140
So in this case, generally,
we're talking about Congress

00:05:44.140 --> 00:05:46.360
or sometimes the state legislatures.

00:05:46.360 --> 00:05:48.420
But by the same token, you can't argue

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:51.780
that small-R republican
values and principles

00:05:51.780 --> 00:05:53.540
are necessarily good.

00:05:53.540 --> 00:05:54.870
And in fact, some of the reasons

00:05:54.870 --> 00:05:56.470
why the United States incorporated

00:05:56.470 --> 00:05:59.120
some of small-R republican ideals

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:01.060
into the system of government

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:04.830
are no longer valid or
helpful in this day and age.

00:06:04.830 --> 00:06:07.290
For example, after the
presidential election in 1800,

00:06:07.290 --> 00:06:09.610
which was depicted in
the musical "Hamilton",

00:06:09.610 --> 00:06:11.130
after that resulted in a tie

00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:13.730
between Thomas Jefferson and Aaron Burr,

00:06:13.730 --> 00:06:16.580
the 12th Amendment was ratified in 1804,

00:06:16.580 --> 00:06:19.160
which stipulates that each
elector must talk less,

00:06:19.160 --> 00:06:21.920
smile more, and cast a distinct vote

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:23.610
for president and vice president

00:06:23.610 --> 00:06:25.900
instead of two votes for president.

00:06:25.900 --> 00:06:28.130
It also requires a person
to receive a majority

00:06:28.130 --> 00:06:30.140
of the electoral votes for vice-president

00:06:30.140 --> 00:06:32.160
for that person to be
elected vice-president

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:34.000
by the Electoral College.

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:35.350
And arguably that made the country

00:06:35.350 --> 00:06:37.230
more democratic and less republican,

00:06:37.230 --> 00:06:39.340
but it fixed some of
the unworkable aspects

00:06:39.340 --> 00:06:42.340
of the original
constitutional system in 1800.

00:06:42.340 --> 00:06:44.610
- And that's just the tip
of the political iceberg

00:06:44.610 --> 00:06:47.140
of how individuals' voting
has shifted in our country.

00:06:47.140 --> 00:06:48.910
- Well, if it isn't Matt
from "Extra Credits"!

00:06:48.910 --> 00:06:50.730
What brings you to my side of YouTube?

00:06:50.730 --> 00:06:53.140
- Well, I overheard you talking
about the Electoral College

00:06:53.140 --> 00:06:54.980
and how it hasn't changed or adapted much

00:06:54.980 --> 00:06:55.980
since America's founding.

00:06:55.980 --> 00:06:57.570
- Wait, this video isn't done yet.

00:06:57.570 --> 00:06:58.460
How did you hear me?

00:06:58.460 --> 00:06:59.360
- Nevermind that.

00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:00.750
You know what has changed though?

00:07:00.750 --> 00:07:03.900
How we vote, like what
Americans physically did

00:07:03.900 --> 00:07:05.930
throughout our history
when we went to the polls

00:07:05.930 --> 00:07:08.470
to choose who each state's
electors would support.

00:07:08.470 --> 00:07:10.240
For instance, back in colonial times

00:07:10.240 --> 00:07:11.640
and just after the revolution,

00:07:11.640 --> 00:07:13.290
you could drunkenly declare your vote

00:07:13.290 --> 00:07:14.750
in public at a carnival.

00:07:14.750 --> 00:07:16.580
Ah, the golden age of voting.

00:07:16.580 --> 00:07:19.630
But by the 1850s, most states
had expanded the electorate

00:07:19.630 --> 00:07:22.090
to the point that voice
voting wasn't practical.

00:07:22.090 --> 00:07:25.320
During America's first
election in 1788-89,

00:07:25.320 --> 00:07:29.410
the popular vote totaled only 43,782.

00:07:29.410 --> 00:07:31.670
But by the election of 1856,

00:07:31.670 --> 00:07:34.110
the popular vote was well over 4 million.

00:07:34.110 --> 00:07:36.570
Voting technology had
to change to keep pace,

00:07:36.570 --> 00:07:38.200
so we shifted to paper ballots

00:07:38.200 --> 00:07:40.000
and that's when things got really weird,

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:42.180
like how the parties used
to print their own ballots,

00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:45.110
not the state, which totally
never messed up anything.

00:07:45.110 --> 00:07:47.030
In fact, we made an entire video

00:07:47.030 --> 00:07:48.520
about all of this and more

00:07:48.520 --> 00:07:50.040
over on the Extra Credits channel.

00:07:50.040 --> 00:07:51.220
And once you've finished up here,

00:07:51.220 --> 00:07:53.240
we'd love for all of you
to come check it out.

00:07:53.240 --> 00:07:54.090
- Sounds good to me.

00:07:54.090 --> 00:07:55.530
We'll see you over there later, Matt.

00:07:55.530 --> 00:07:56.363
- Awesome.

00:07:56.363 --> 00:07:57.400
Cool if I just wait here?

00:07:57.400 --> 00:08:00.194
- Actually, I'm kind of in the
middle of this video, so...

00:08:00.194 --> 00:08:03.226
- Right, right right, yeah,
totally, no, understood.

00:08:03.226 --> 00:08:04.059
- Erm...

00:08:04.991 --> 00:08:06.960
- I, I, I mean, I could just play Phoenix

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:08.750
right in the corner until you're done or-

00:08:08.750 --> 00:08:10.300
- Bailiff, tackle this man!

00:08:10.300 --> 00:08:11.500
Get him out of my court.

00:08:12.380 --> 00:08:15.460
- [Matt] He said the
thing, totally worth it.

00:08:15.460 --> 00:08:20.460
- Yeah, so, things got even
more small-D democratic in 1913,

00:08:20.710 --> 00:08:22.710
when the 17th Amendment was ratified.

00:08:22.710 --> 00:08:25.230
The Constitution originally
required state legislatures

00:08:25.230 --> 00:08:28.050
to choose state senators
rather than voters.

00:08:28.050 --> 00:08:31.050
A very republican principle,
but not very democratic.

00:08:31.050 --> 00:08:33.540
However, this became contentious
when state legislatures

00:08:33.540 --> 00:08:35.570
fought amongst themselves
over who to appoint

00:08:35.570 --> 00:08:38.090
and Senate seats started
going to the highest bidders.

00:08:38.090 --> 00:08:40.050
The 17th Amendment gave people the right

00:08:40.050 --> 00:08:41.850
to vote for their senators directly

00:08:41.850 --> 00:08:43.940
instead of the state's legislatures.

00:08:43.940 --> 00:08:45.390
This is called a direct election,

00:08:45.390 --> 00:08:47.020
where the people choose who's in office,

00:08:47.020 --> 00:08:48.740
but America is still a republic

00:08:48.740 --> 00:08:50.810
because they have official senators

00:08:50.810 --> 00:08:53.110
who actually vote on the laws themselves.

00:08:53.110 --> 00:08:55.793
But back to our story and
the Electoral College.

00:08:55.793 --> 00:08:58.330
After 2016, the Supreme
Court ruled in a case

00:08:58.330 --> 00:09:00.180
called Chiafalo versus Washington.

00:09:00.180 --> 00:09:02.500
The state may enforce an elector's pledge

00:09:02.500 --> 00:09:04.960
to support the winning party's nominee

00:09:04.960 --> 00:09:07.630
and the state voters choice for president.

00:09:07.630 --> 00:09:09.950
Colorado's faithless electors law mandated

00:09:09.950 --> 00:09:12.130
that an elector be removed immediately.

00:09:12.130 --> 00:09:13.630
The Washington State law required

00:09:13.630 --> 00:09:15.800
that electors be fined $1,000 dollars

00:09:15.800 --> 00:09:18.580
if they contradicted the
popular vote of that state.

00:09:18.580 --> 00:09:20.220
The Supreme Court said
that this was acceptable

00:09:20.220 --> 00:09:22.720
because Article II includes
only the instruction

00:09:22.720 --> 00:09:24.610
to each state to appoint electors

00:09:24.610 --> 00:09:26.410
and the 12th Amendment only sets out

00:09:26.410 --> 00:09:28.150
the electors' voting procedures.

00:09:28.150 --> 00:09:30.110
The Constitution doesn't
contain any language

00:09:30.110 --> 00:09:32.170
indicating electors have discretion,

00:09:32.170 --> 00:09:34.360
so if a state individually says

00:09:34.360 --> 00:09:36.940
that there can be no faithless
electors in that state

00:09:36.940 --> 00:09:39.520
then that state won't have
any faithless electors

00:09:39.520 --> 00:09:42.560
and states can remove
these electors on the spot.

00:09:42.560 --> 00:09:45.840
But Chiafalo did not
outlaw faithless electors.

00:09:45.840 --> 00:09:49.000
33 states and DC require electors to vote

00:09:49.000 --> 00:09:50.060
for a pledged candidate.

00:09:50.060 --> 00:09:52.700
But most of these states don't
penalize a faithless selector

00:09:52.700 --> 00:09:54.300
like Washington or Colorado.

00:09:54.300 --> 00:09:56.020
In many of these states,
the faithless electors

00:09:56.020 --> 00:09:59.170
who break his or her pledge
simply get away with it.

00:09:59.170 --> 00:10:01.960
And the deviant vote is
still counted or cast.

00:10:01.960 --> 00:10:03.060
This includes some of the states

00:10:03.060 --> 00:10:04.690
where the 2020 presidential election

00:10:04.690 --> 00:10:06.360
is expected to be closest.

00:10:06.360 --> 00:10:08.200
In Florida, Ohio, and Nevada

00:10:08.200 --> 00:10:10.210
electors are supposed to
vote for the candidate

00:10:10.210 --> 00:10:12.290
who won the majority of
the state's popular vote

00:10:12.290 --> 00:10:13.610
or for the candidate of the party

00:10:13.610 --> 00:10:15.440
that nominated that elector.

00:10:15.440 --> 00:10:18.980
But if they do their own thing,
then that vote still counts.

00:10:18.980 --> 00:10:20.910
And then there are major
battleground states

00:10:20.910 --> 00:10:23.160
like Pennsylvania, which
actually don't require electors

00:10:23.160 --> 00:10:25.860
to support the winner of
the state's popular vote.

00:10:25.860 --> 00:10:28.010
Could that elector change their vote?

00:10:28.010 --> 00:10:30.600
Well, under current Pennsylvania law, yes.

00:10:30.600 --> 00:10:33.700
But faithless electors actually
don't come up that often

00:10:33.700 --> 00:10:37.003
and fingers crossed, so far
they've never been decisive

00:10:37.003 --> 00:10:39.030
in a presidential election.

00:10:39.030 --> 00:10:40.450
But there is one scenario

00:10:40.450 --> 00:10:42.850
that hasn't ever been
particularly relevant

00:10:42.850 --> 00:10:46.080
that may become important
in this particular election.

00:10:46.080 --> 00:10:48.570
Some refer to it as The Doomsday Scenario,

00:10:48.570 --> 00:10:50.720
which is that there's
actually room for states

00:10:50.720 --> 00:10:53.830
to dramatically change
how electors must vote.

00:10:53.830 --> 00:10:56.280
Any state legislature
could enact legislation

00:10:56.280 --> 00:10:59.590
that would change how the
governor appoints electors.

00:10:59.590 --> 00:11:01.270
And a state legislature could require

00:11:01.270 --> 00:11:03.040
that its electors vote for a candidate

00:11:03.040 --> 00:11:06.680
who did not receive a majority
of the state's popular vote.

00:11:06.680 --> 00:11:08.930
Then there's no Constitutional
provision or federal law

00:11:08.930 --> 00:11:10.570
that requires electors to vote

00:11:10.570 --> 00:11:12.610
according to the results
of the popular vote

00:11:12.610 --> 00:11:13.590
in that particular state.

00:11:13.590 --> 00:11:15.830
So if, for example, a
Republican legislature

00:11:15.830 --> 00:11:18.400
that's capital-R Republican in this case,

00:11:18.400 --> 00:11:21.520
in, let's say, Pennsylvania
decided to change the law

00:11:21.520 --> 00:11:23.160
right before the election

00:11:23.160 --> 00:11:26.520
to change who the electors
vote for, could they?

00:11:26.520 --> 00:11:28.190
Well, very possibly.

00:11:28.190 --> 00:11:30.740
And this loophole is allegedly
already being explored

00:11:30.740 --> 00:11:33.450
by the Trump campaign, which
is reportedly in discussions

00:11:33.450 --> 00:11:34.920
with state and national Republicans

00:11:34.920 --> 00:11:36.947
to quote "bypass election results

00:11:36.947 --> 00:11:39.077
"and appoint loyal electors
in battleground states

00:11:39.077 --> 00:11:42.019
"where Republicans hold
the legislative majority."

00:11:42.019 --> 00:11:44.330
And this isn't just hypothetical.

00:11:44.330 --> 00:11:47.122
Lawrence Tabas, chair of the
Pennsylvania Republican Party

00:11:47.122 --> 00:11:49.470
also told The Atlantic
that he has discussed

00:11:49.470 --> 00:11:51.750
the direct appointment of
electors with the Trump campaign

00:11:51.750 --> 00:11:53.587
saying quote, "I've mentioned it to them

00:11:53.587 --> 00:11:55.680
"and I hope that they're
thinking about it, too."

00:11:55.680 --> 00:11:58.480
But frankly, this seems
like an unlikely scenario.

00:11:58.480 --> 00:12:00.530
And if any political party were successful

00:12:00.530 --> 00:12:03.110
in creating such an anti-democratic policy

00:12:03.110 --> 00:12:05.780
that were able to counter
the will of the people

00:12:05.780 --> 00:12:10.170
and if that policy were decisive
in a presidential election,

00:12:10.170 --> 00:12:12.220
there would probably be
riots in the streets.

00:12:12.220 --> 00:12:15.010
But with the current
Electoral College system

00:12:15.010 --> 00:12:18.510
and this small-R republican principle,

00:12:18.510 --> 00:12:20.630
these kind of scenarios are possible

00:12:20.630 --> 00:12:22.340
and states can pass a law

00:12:22.340 --> 00:12:24.130
changing the way that electors are chosen

00:12:24.130 --> 00:12:25.550
because the Supreme Court has ruled

00:12:25.550 --> 00:12:26.850
that the people have no right

00:12:26.850 --> 00:12:29.480
to select the electors themselves.

00:12:29.480 --> 00:12:31.270
So a state could change the law

00:12:31.270 --> 00:12:33.930
to take back the people's
right to select the electors

00:12:33.930 --> 00:12:37.770
and use some other policy
rather than the popular vote

00:12:37.770 --> 00:12:40.010
of that particular state.

00:12:40.010 --> 00:12:42.150
So let's talk about how
these electoral votes

00:12:42.150 --> 00:12:44.560
in the Electoral College are apportioned.

00:12:44.560 --> 00:12:45.870
Each state's electoral votes

00:12:45.870 --> 00:12:48.060
are equal to the number of
representatives and senators

00:12:48.060 --> 00:12:49.840
the state has in Congress.

00:12:49.840 --> 00:12:52.770
House seats apportionments
are based on population

00:12:52.770 --> 00:12:54.860
and are reapportioned every
decade after the census,

00:12:54.860 --> 00:12:57.260
which is why the census is so important.

00:12:57.260 --> 00:13:00.220
Every state is guaranteed at
least one seat in the House

00:13:00.220 --> 00:13:02.730
and then that number goes
up based on the population,

00:13:02.730 --> 00:13:04.780
and of course, as you
remember from Civics class,

00:13:04.780 --> 00:13:08.580
every state has two and only
two seats in the Senate.

00:13:08.580 --> 00:13:09.960
So the Constitution specifies

00:13:09.960 --> 00:13:11.610
how many electors every state gets.

00:13:11.610 --> 00:13:13.220
Pretty simple math, the number of senators

00:13:13.220 --> 00:13:14.860
plus the number of Congress people.

00:13:14.860 --> 00:13:16.270
But the Constitution is silent

00:13:16.270 --> 00:13:18.710
on just about everything else.

00:13:18.710 --> 00:13:20.340
The Constitution leaves
it up to the states,

00:13:20.340 --> 00:13:21.870
how they're going to apportion

00:13:21.870 --> 00:13:23.770
those electors in the Electoral College,

00:13:23.770 --> 00:13:25.800
and on what basis states
can make the decision

00:13:25.800 --> 00:13:28.520
as to whether to grant all of
the electors for that state,

00:13:28.520 --> 00:13:30.210
or just a portion of them.

00:13:30.210 --> 00:13:33.030
It's probably not surprising
that, by the election of 1800,

00:13:33.030 --> 00:13:36.060
the states had decided how
they were going to apportion

00:13:36.060 --> 00:13:37.590
the electors in the Electoral College

00:13:37.590 --> 00:13:39.070
and had more or less all shifted

00:13:39.070 --> 00:13:40.490
to a winner-take-all system,

00:13:40.490 --> 00:13:42.690
also known as a First
Past the Post system,

00:13:42.690 --> 00:13:45.100
because that's how most
US elections occurred.

00:13:45.100 --> 00:13:47.300
I'll note parenthetically
that most modern democracies

00:13:47.300 --> 00:13:49.040
use a proportional representation system

00:13:49.040 --> 00:13:49.873
in their legislature,

00:13:49.873 --> 00:13:52.940
but that's a can of worms for
an entirely different video.

00:13:52.940 --> 00:13:54.980
Now, most states have a
winner-take-all system

00:13:54.980 --> 00:13:57.040
that awards the votes
of a state's electors

00:13:57.040 --> 00:13:58.210
to the presidential candidate

00:13:58.210 --> 00:14:00.560
who obtains the most votes in that state.

00:14:00.560 --> 00:14:02.070
Maine and Nebraska, however,

00:14:02.070 --> 00:14:04.070
have enacted the
congressional district method

00:14:04.070 --> 00:14:06.080
which allocates one electoral vote

00:14:06.080 --> 00:14:08.010
to the winner of the popular vote

00:14:08.010 --> 00:14:10.086
in each state-drawn district.

00:14:10.086 --> 00:14:11.550
In winner-take-all states,

00:14:11.550 --> 00:14:14.180
which are the vast majority
of American states,

00:14:14.180 --> 00:14:16.830
all of the electoral votes
available to that state

00:14:16.830 --> 00:14:19.070
go to the winner of the popular vote

00:14:19.070 --> 00:14:20.250
in that particular state.

00:14:20.250 --> 00:14:22.860
So if a candidate wins a
simple majority in the state

00:14:22.860 --> 00:14:25.540
then 100% of the electoral
votes in that state

00:14:25.540 --> 00:14:28.190
go to that candidate,
regardless of whether they win

00:14:28.190 --> 00:14:30.540
with one vote or with 1 million votes.

00:14:30.540 --> 00:14:32.670
And that's how it works in 48 states

00:14:32.670 --> 00:14:34.210
and the District of Columbia.

00:14:34.210 --> 00:14:36.650
This is a system that America
inherited from the British

00:14:36.650 --> 00:14:39.430
that's often referred to as
the First Past the Post system.

00:14:39.430 --> 00:14:40.263
But it's important to note

00:14:40.263 --> 00:14:42.020
that not all of the
framers of the Constitution

00:14:42.020 --> 00:14:44.050
actually agreed with the
winner-take-all system

00:14:44.050 --> 00:14:46.490
that the states adopted
in the Electoral College.

00:14:46.490 --> 00:14:49.080
And in fact, by 1823, James Madison,

00:14:49.080 --> 00:14:50.380
the father of the Constitution,

00:14:50.380 --> 00:14:52.280
railed against winner-take-all system.

00:14:52.280 --> 00:14:55.420
He actually argued for more
granular district-based system

00:14:55.420 --> 00:14:57.570
if not a national popular vote in general.

00:14:57.570 --> 00:14:59.900
And in fact, he proposed an
amendment to the Constitution

00:14:59.900 --> 00:15:03.210
to do away with the construction
of the Electoral College,

00:15:03.210 --> 00:15:05.110
as it more or less exists today.

00:15:05.110 --> 00:15:06.910
But it's received a lot of criticism

00:15:06.910 --> 00:15:09.120
because there can often be a lot of people

00:15:09.120 --> 00:15:10.270
in the minority of that state

00:15:10.270 --> 00:15:12.482
whose votes are effectively uncounted.

00:15:12.482 --> 00:15:15.220
There are a lot of
Republicans in California,

00:15:15.220 --> 00:15:18.170
but effectively they have
no effect on the election

00:15:18.170 --> 00:15:20.490
because there will be
a majority of Democrats

00:15:20.490 --> 00:15:23.880
voting in California and all
of California's electoral votes

00:15:23.880 --> 00:15:25.650
will go to Democrats.

00:15:25.650 --> 00:15:27.760
There are a lot of Democrats in Texas,

00:15:27.760 --> 00:15:30.560
but effectively, those
votes aren't counted either

00:15:30.560 --> 00:15:33.970
because if the majority vote
for Republican in Texas,

00:15:33.970 --> 00:15:35.442
then all of Texas's electoral votes

00:15:35.442 --> 00:15:37.970
will go to the Republican candidate.

00:15:37.970 --> 00:15:40.250
And coupled with the fact
that smaller states receive

00:15:40.250 --> 00:15:43.610
more electoral votes in
comparison to their population,

00:15:43.610 --> 00:15:45.160
this is how you can get the scenario

00:15:45.160 --> 00:15:47.900
where a candidate receives
less of the popular vote

00:15:47.900 --> 00:15:49.600
but wins in the Electoral College.

00:15:49.600 --> 00:15:52.090
A lot of people refer to
this as gerrymandering,

00:15:52.090 --> 00:15:53.490
but that's not quite right.

00:15:53.490 --> 00:15:56.576
Gerrymandering refers to
specifically and intentionally

00:15:56.576 --> 00:16:00.650
creating weird districts
to create a certain outcome

00:16:00.650 --> 00:16:02.040
in apportioning the population,

00:16:02.040 --> 00:16:04.930
whereas this is just
an issue that comes up

00:16:04.930 --> 00:16:06.760
with the way that the states exist,

00:16:06.760 --> 00:16:09.510
and there's no way to change the outline

00:16:09.510 --> 00:16:11.030
and the borders of the states themselves.

00:16:11.030 --> 00:16:12.830
And this is just a by-product of the fact

00:16:12.830 --> 00:16:14.240
that it's a winner take all system

00:16:14.240 --> 00:16:16.120
in almost all of the states.

00:16:16.120 --> 00:16:17.360
Interestingly, the argument

00:16:17.360 --> 00:16:19.970
that the Electoral College
benefits the small states

00:16:19.970 --> 00:16:22.043
and that's the reason it was
adopted in the first place

00:16:22.043 --> 00:16:23.730
is largely apocryphal.

00:16:23.730 --> 00:16:25.210
In reality, it has more to do do with

00:16:25.210 --> 00:16:28.420
the southern slave-holding
states wanting to retain power

00:16:28.420 --> 00:16:30.330
related to the Three-fifths Compromise.

00:16:30.330 --> 00:16:32.530
But since the adoption of
the winner-take-all system,

00:16:32.530 --> 00:16:35.060
small states have for the
most part been ignored

00:16:35.060 --> 00:16:36.680
unless they were battleground states,

00:16:36.680 --> 00:16:38.620
which we'll get to in just a bit.

00:16:38.620 --> 00:16:39.810
But this raises the question

00:16:39.810 --> 00:16:41.640
as to why more states don't do

00:16:41.640 --> 00:16:43.400
what Maine and Nebraska do.

00:16:43.400 --> 00:16:45.670
Well, it's partially a prisoner's dilemma

00:16:45.670 --> 00:16:47.290
and it's kind of about power.

00:16:47.290 --> 00:16:49.150
When the swing state
awards the maximum number

00:16:49.150 --> 00:16:51.660
of Electoral College votes
to the statewide winner,

00:16:51.660 --> 00:16:55.090
it ensures extra attention
from presidential candidates.

00:16:55.090 --> 00:16:56.770
That's why Florida and Pennsylvania

00:16:56.770 --> 00:16:58.520
see a lot of action from the candidates.

00:16:58.520 --> 00:17:01.000
The stakes are too high to ignore,

00:17:01.000 --> 00:17:02.870
the fact that Florida could go either way

00:17:02.870 --> 00:17:04.440
and that's a lot of electoral votes

00:17:04.440 --> 00:17:05.690
that could swing an election.

00:17:05.690 --> 00:17:08.120
For spectator states, the
winner-take-all systems

00:17:08.120 --> 00:17:10.460
offer maximum power to
the candidate favored

00:17:10.460 --> 00:17:12.680
by the clear majority of
the state's population.

00:17:12.680 --> 00:17:15.630
And this also gives the state
more cachet than it would

00:17:15.630 --> 00:17:17.750
if it were splitting its votes.

00:17:17.750 --> 00:17:19.450
And parenthetically,
that's one of the reasons

00:17:19.450 --> 00:17:22.010
why you always hear
presidential candidates

00:17:22.010 --> 00:17:24.160
have a stance on things like ethanol.

00:17:24.160 --> 00:17:26.260
- Pure American ethanol.

00:17:26.260 --> 00:17:29.639
- Because corn, which is the
product that leads to ethanol,

00:17:29.639 --> 00:17:33.090
is really big in Iowa, where
there is an early caucus

00:17:33.090 --> 00:17:35.220
and there is a lot of attention paid

00:17:35.220 --> 00:17:37.760
to campaigning in Iowa

00:17:37.760 --> 00:17:39.580
and winning the votes of Iowans,

00:17:39.580 --> 00:17:42.490
even though it's a very
low population state.

00:17:42.490 --> 00:17:44.480
But let's talk about
this over-representation

00:17:44.480 --> 00:17:46.350
of less populous states.

00:17:46.350 --> 00:17:49.030
America has always tried
to thread the needle,

00:17:49.030 --> 00:17:52.820
between trying to give
every citizen an equal vote,

00:17:52.820 --> 00:17:55.330
the idea of one person, one vote,

00:17:55.330 --> 00:17:58.330
while also trying to create a
system of checks and balances

00:17:58.330 --> 00:18:00.861
that doesn't let more populous states

00:18:00.861 --> 00:18:03.400
completely dominate
all of the other states

00:18:03.400 --> 00:18:04.960
or, originally, the colonies.

00:18:04.960 --> 00:18:07.430
And one of the features,
or one of the bugs,

00:18:07.430 --> 00:18:09.830
of the Electoral College,
depending on who you ask,

00:18:09.830 --> 00:18:11.190
is that it's supposed to guarantee

00:18:11.190 --> 00:18:13.840
that the populous states
can't dominate in an election,

00:18:13.840 --> 00:18:17.040
but that also creates a
disparity in representation

00:18:17.040 --> 00:18:18.950
because we have to go back to remembering

00:18:18.950 --> 00:18:20.750
that the way that the
Electoral College is set up

00:18:20.750 --> 00:18:23.560
is that every state gets
at least three votes.

00:18:23.560 --> 00:18:25.000
One based on a representative,

00:18:25.000 --> 00:18:26.690
because the minimum
number of representatives

00:18:26.690 --> 00:18:30.960
in the House is one, and
every state gets two senators,

00:18:30.960 --> 00:18:32.330
and that's the same regardless

00:18:32.330 --> 00:18:34.350
of the population of the state.

00:18:34.350 --> 00:18:35.920
But when you take the
population of the state

00:18:35.920 --> 00:18:38.130
and then divide it the number of electors,

00:18:38.130 --> 00:18:40.220
what you get is a wildly different number.

00:18:40.220 --> 00:18:41.350
So while California has

00:18:41.350 --> 00:18:44.480
one electoral vote per 712,000 people,

00:18:44.480 --> 00:18:46.730
Wyoming, the state with the
fewest people in the country

00:18:46.730 --> 00:18:50.170
has one electoral vote per 195,000 people.

00:18:50.170 --> 00:18:51.810
And that's because each state must have

00:18:51.810 --> 00:18:53.240
at least three electoral votes.

00:18:53.240 --> 00:18:55.080
So Wyoming has three electoral votes

00:18:55.080 --> 00:18:57.560
and only 532,000 citizens.

00:18:57.560 --> 00:18:59.650
California is the most
misrepresented state

00:18:59.650 --> 00:19:00.894
in the Electoral College.

00:19:00.894 --> 00:19:03.530
California is home to
12% of all Americans,

00:19:03.530 --> 00:19:05.960
but has just 10% of the electoral votes.

00:19:05.960 --> 00:19:07.750
And while that might not seem like much,

00:19:07.750 --> 00:19:09.250
when you spread that effect out

00:19:09.250 --> 00:19:10.300
throughout the entire country,

00:19:10.300 --> 00:19:12.560
you can get wildly disparate results.

00:19:12.560 --> 00:19:14.670
And although California
has gained electoral votes

00:19:14.670 --> 00:19:16.350
as its population has increased

00:19:16.350 --> 00:19:18.190
and it's been allocated
more representatives,

00:19:18.190 --> 00:19:20.480
this hasn't been enough
to address this disparity.

00:19:20.480 --> 00:19:22.940
Because population growth tends to outpace

00:19:22.940 --> 00:19:24.583
Electoral College representation,

00:19:24.583 --> 00:19:27.200
which changes once every 10 years

00:19:27.200 --> 00:19:28.798
when the census rolls around.

00:19:28.798 --> 00:19:30.060
Even after the census,

00:19:30.060 --> 00:19:32.120
a state doesn't earn new electoral votes

00:19:32.120 --> 00:19:35.620
until it's gained approximately
700,000 new residents.

00:19:35.620 --> 00:19:37.890
The result is that a
state or metropolitan area

00:19:37.890 --> 00:19:39.764
can add hundreds of thousands of new votes

00:19:39.764 --> 00:19:41.460
without gaining any greater

00:19:41.460 --> 00:19:43.150
Electoral College representation.

00:19:43.150 --> 00:19:44.520
And this is exactly what's happening

00:19:44.520 --> 00:19:45.820
in both Texas and California.

00:19:45.820 --> 00:19:48.280
They can add 640,000 new residents,

00:19:48.280 --> 00:19:51.680
which is actually more than the
entire population of Wyoming

00:19:51.680 --> 00:19:54.720
and still not gain a single
additional electoral vote.

00:19:54.720 --> 00:19:58.190
In fact, 640,000 is more than
the total voting population

00:19:58.190 --> 00:20:01.050
of six of the smaller,
less populous states.

00:20:01.050 --> 00:20:02.610
As a result the Electoral College

00:20:02.610 --> 00:20:04.670
tends to penalize population centers.

00:20:04.670 --> 00:20:06.750
And in a weird quirk of fate,

00:20:06.750 --> 00:20:08.700
population centers tend to be pretty blue

00:20:08.700 --> 00:20:11.510
while rural communities
they tend to be pretty red.

00:20:11.510 --> 00:20:14.910
But there's no reason why
that's necessarily the case.

00:20:14.910 --> 00:20:17.010
Of course, it would be
foolish not to acknowledge

00:20:17.010 --> 00:20:20.060
the partisan implications
of the Electoral College.

00:20:20.060 --> 00:20:21.640
This issue has turned partisan

00:20:21.640 --> 00:20:24.530
because the same party has
benefited twice, the Republicans,

00:20:24.530 --> 00:20:26.990
and the same party has
been disadvantaged twice.

00:20:26.990 --> 00:20:28.410
Hillary Clinton won the popular vote

00:20:28.410 --> 00:20:29.590
by almost 3 million votes

00:20:29.590 --> 00:20:31.190
and Al Gore won the popular vote

00:20:31.190 --> 00:20:33.140
by almost half a million votes.

00:20:33.140 --> 00:20:35.010
Now, if demographic changes continue

00:20:35.010 --> 00:20:36.470
in the direction that they're going,

00:20:36.470 --> 00:20:38.780
Texas will eventually turn blue.

00:20:38.780 --> 00:20:40.640
And if that happens,
Republicans would effectively

00:20:40.640 --> 00:20:42.760
have no path to ever win the presidency,

00:20:42.760 --> 00:20:45.380
ironically, due to the Electoral College.

00:20:45.380 --> 00:20:47.320
So for those supporters
of the Electoral College

00:20:47.320 --> 00:20:49.320
ask yourself if you would still support it

00:20:49.320 --> 00:20:52.590
if it precluded Republicans
from ever getting the presidency

00:20:52.590 --> 00:20:55.990
or at a bare minimum, allowing
Texas to decide the fate

00:20:55.990 --> 00:20:58.060
of effectively every presidential election

00:20:58.060 --> 00:20:59.510
for the foreseeable future.

00:20:59.510 --> 00:21:02.320
And this leads to what
people often refer to

00:21:02.320 --> 00:21:03.910
as spectator states.

00:21:03.910 --> 00:21:05.710
Although some people claim
that the Electoral College

00:21:05.710 --> 00:21:08.080
protects rural voters or rural states,

00:21:08.080 --> 00:21:10.290
this is only partially true.

00:21:10.290 --> 00:21:13.010
Presidential campaigns still
don't tend to make stops

00:21:13.010 --> 00:21:15.900
in Wyoming, North or
South Dakota, or Montana.

00:21:15.900 --> 00:21:17.760
So generally speaking,
the Electoral College

00:21:17.760 --> 00:21:19.680
tends to create a disparity in campaigning

00:21:19.680 --> 00:21:22.810
between spectator states and swing states.

00:21:22.810 --> 00:21:25.810
In 2016, for example, most
of the presidential campaigns

00:21:25.810 --> 00:21:27.040
took place in just four states:

00:21:27.040 --> 00:21:29.870
Ohio, Florida, Pennsylvania,
and North Carolina,

00:21:29.870 --> 00:21:31.370
while two thirds of the campaign events

00:21:31.370 --> 00:21:33.670
took place in just six states.

00:21:33.670 --> 00:21:35.250
The election was decided by the votes

00:21:35.250 --> 00:21:37.720
of around 80,000 people in three states.

00:21:37.720 --> 00:21:39.610
And the two most populated states,

00:21:39.610 --> 00:21:42.175
California and Texas,
are spectator states,

00:21:42.175 --> 00:21:46.300
as are 12 of the 13 least populous states.

00:21:46.300 --> 00:21:47.210
These states are ignored

00:21:47.210 --> 00:21:49.150
because the outcomes
are already determined

00:21:49.150 --> 00:21:51.120
since the vast majority
of voters in those states

00:21:51.120 --> 00:21:52.900
prefer one political party.

00:21:52.900 --> 00:21:54.390
The swing states are swing states

00:21:54.390 --> 00:21:56.360
because they have the most even split

00:21:56.360 --> 00:21:58.500
between Republicans and Democratic voters.

00:21:58.500 --> 00:22:00.180
And this leads to some weird outcomes

00:22:00.180 --> 00:22:02.050
because 48 states and Washington, DC,

00:22:02.050 --> 00:22:04.550
award their votes on a
winner-take-all basis, which means

00:22:04.550 --> 00:22:07.060
that it's possible for a
candidate to win the presidency

00:22:07.060 --> 00:22:10.150
with around 23% of the
national popular vote.

00:22:10.150 --> 00:22:12.000
And that may not happen in 2020,

00:22:12.000 --> 00:22:15.280
but the fact that it can
happen is always alarming.

00:22:15.280 --> 00:22:17.310
And of course in 2016, Hillary Clinton

00:22:17.310 --> 00:22:19.580
got over 2 million more
votes than Donald Trump

00:22:19.580 --> 00:22:23.410
but lost the Electoral College 306 to 232.

00:22:23.410 --> 00:22:25.980
In raw votes, it was the
largest popular vote lead

00:22:25.980 --> 00:22:28.550
in history for a candidate
who lost the election.

00:22:28.550 --> 00:22:30.340
If Donald Trump wins in 2020,

00:22:30.340 --> 00:22:32.260
it could be the first
time in American history

00:22:32.260 --> 00:22:34.280
that a president was elected twice

00:22:34.280 --> 00:22:37.330
without winning the majority
of the vote in either case.

00:22:37.330 --> 00:22:38.920
So let's talk about some of the proposals

00:22:38.920 --> 00:22:41.340
that people have suggested
to fix some of the flaws

00:22:41.340 --> 00:22:42.930
of the Electoral College.

00:22:42.930 --> 00:22:44.440
In the current Electoral College system,

00:22:44.440 --> 00:22:46.160
the presidency is awarded to the candidate

00:22:46.160 --> 00:22:50.820
who wins at least 270 of the
538 available electoral votes.

00:22:50.820 --> 00:22:52.700
The Constitution gives state legislatures

00:22:52.700 --> 00:22:55.690
the right to choose how
presidential electors are chosen.

00:22:55.690 --> 00:22:57.310
Since the 19th century, each state,

00:22:57.310 --> 00:22:59.240
with the exceptions of Maine
and Nebraska of course,

00:22:59.240 --> 00:23:00.920
have awarded the electoral vote

00:23:00.920 --> 00:23:03.350
to the winner of the
popular vote in that state.

00:23:03.350 --> 00:23:04.940
But some people have
proposed what's called

00:23:04.940 --> 00:23:07.120
the National Popular Vote Compact

00:23:07.120 --> 00:23:09.430
and under a national popular vote system,

00:23:09.430 --> 00:23:11.800
states would commit to
award their electoral votes

00:23:11.800 --> 00:23:14.280
to the winner of the national popular vote

00:23:14.280 --> 00:23:16.843
instead of the state popular vote.

00:23:16.843 --> 00:23:19.200
The National Popular Vote
Compact has been passed

00:23:19.200 --> 00:23:22.410
by 15 states and the District of Columbia.

00:23:22.410 --> 00:23:25.070
And since there isn't a
constitutional requirement

00:23:25.070 --> 00:23:26.614
as to how these votes are allocated,

00:23:26.614 --> 00:23:30.040
so long as these states
agree to this system,

00:23:30.040 --> 00:23:31.410
then it's constitutional,

00:23:31.410 --> 00:23:33.750
it doesn't require an additional
constitutional amendment.

00:23:33.750 --> 00:23:35.430
Of course, a lot of
people would be worried

00:23:35.430 --> 00:23:37.730
if a individual state decided

00:23:37.730 --> 00:23:41.120
to allocate its votes based
on the national popular vote

00:23:41.120 --> 00:23:42.810
without other states going along with it,

00:23:42.810 --> 00:23:46.260
or if a state decided
to award its electors

00:23:46.260 --> 00:23:48.520
in proportion to the proportion

00:23:48.520 --> 00:23:50.510
of the national popular vote,

00:23:50.510 --> 00:23:52.063
which is one of the genius aspects

00:23:52.063 --> 00:23:54.170
of the National Popular Vote Compact

00:23:54.170 --> 00:23:56.230
which is that it would only go into effect

00:23:56.230 --> 00:23:59.499
when states controlling at
least 270 electoral votes

00:23:59.499 --> 00:24:01.270
have joined.

00:24:01.270 --> 00:24:03.440
In the election after
that threshold is reached,

00:24:03.440 --> 00:24:05.530
the national popular
vote states would ensure

00:24:05.530 --> 00:24:07.314
that the winner of the
national popular vote

00:24:07.314 --> 00:24:08.980
became president.

00:24:08.980 --> 00:24:11.590
While the Compact would not
abolish the Electoral College,

00:24:11.590 --> 00:24:12.930
it would guarantee that the winner

00:24:12.930 --> 00:24:14.150
of the Electoral College vote

00:24:14.150 --> 00:24:16.730
and the popular vote are the same.

00:24:16.730 --> 00:24:18.430
Now, there are certainly some advantages

00:24:18.430 --> 00:24:19.930
to having an Electoral College

00:24:19.930 --> 00:24:22.830
but all of this plotting
underlies the main problem

00:24:22.830 --> 00:24:23.830
with the Electoral College,

00:24:23.830 --> 00:24:24.967
which is that there are a lot of steps

00:24:24.967 --> 00:24:27.090
that lead to litigation and mess

00:24:27.090 --> 00:24:28.410
rather than a clear winner,

00:24:28.410 --> 00:24:32.690
and winners that can lose
the national popular vote.

00:24:32.690 --> 00:24:34.490
The Electoral College
creates the possibility

00:24:34.490 --> 00:24:36.750
of a 269-269 tie vote,

00:24:36.750 --> 00:24:39.782
the possibility of a 23% vote
getter becoming president

00:24:39.782 --> 00:24:41.530
and the possibility of the majority

00:24:41.530 --> 00:24:43.520
being ruled by a minority.

00:24:43.520 --> 00:24:45.070
But I will leave it to you.

00:24:45.070 --> 00:24:46.230
Do you agree with my analysis?

00:24:46.230 --> 00:24:48.090
Do you think that the Electoral
College is a good thing?

00:24:48.090 --> 00:24:50.950
Should it be abolished,
changed, or just left alone?

00:24:50.950 --> 00:24:52.760
Leave your objections in the comments

00:24:52.760 --> 00:24:54.360
and check out Extra Credits' video

00:24:54.360 --> 00:24:56.420
on the history of voting in this country.

00:24:56.420 --> 00:24:59.080
It's really great where I have a cameo,

00:24:59.080 --> 00:25:00.440
and check out this playlist over here

00:25:00.440 --> 00:25:02.570
with all of my other real law reviews

00:25:02.570 --> 00:25:05.330
where I talk about the important
legal issues of the day,

00:25:05.330 --> 00:25:07.330
including lots of stuff
about this election.

00:25:07.330 --> 00:25:10.533
So, click on this playlist
and I'll see you in court.

